:>     And shouldn't peaceful dwarves refrain from doing a furniture 
:>rearrangement on the level?  
:
:Why? 
     Because it's very annoying.  In fact, on an MS-DOS system (at least
on my 10MHz XT), the whole concept of peaceful monsters is very annoying.
A chief reason why I retain a pet as far downward as I can (I wish tripe
were more common.  They really make it tough to keep the dog around).
The friendly types slow down the game as it is (you don't know what it's
like to maneuver around on a level full of gremlins).  I wish they would
disappear after a time (perhaps when they hit the stairs).  Perhaps not
realistic, but quite playable.

:The player doesn't own the level; in fact, he or she is not even native to
:it, and it is in the nature of dwarves etc. to dig for minerals, gems etc. What
:harm does it do you for the level to be dug out (other than the display turning
:ugly)?
     It makes it a lot more difficult to avoid swarms of monsters attacking
you three or four at a time when whole segments of the level are opened up.
Especially when you can only see one square at a time.  I've lost many a
character to waiting in the middle of a room and having three soldier ants
come up and massacre him (since they can move double-speed).  While that's
generally my fault, peaceful dwarves could make that much more difficult to
avoid.
     (I think soldier ants should be moved down a few levels as it is, or at
least swarms of them.)

